C1: {
  "statement": "**C1 · Core · PyTorch — the threshold has no useful gradient.** Build a perceptron as `torch.heaviside(x @ w + b, torch.tensor(1.0))` with `w` requiring gradients, compute a squared error on a few points, and call `backward()`. Report what happens. Then replace the step with `torch.sigmoid(k*(x @ w + b))` for k=1, 10 and 100, and report the gradient norm. Explain why the perceptron rule is not gradient descent on the classification error, and what this means for training networks of threshold units.",
  "plain": "Record the actual result of backward() on torch.heaviside in your installed PyTorch version, including any unsupported-derivative error. Mathematically the hard step is locally constant away from zero and has no ordinary derivative at zero, so it gives no useful ordinary gradient for classification error. With sigmoid(kz), the local derivative is kσ(kz)(1−σ(kz)); it concentrates near z=0 as k grows. Gradients can shrink for points far from zero and can increase near zero; report actual norms rather than requiring a universal monotonic trend. The perceptron update is supervised error correction, not gradient descent on 0/1 error. Hidden-layer training needs usable derivatives, including piecewise derivatives for ReLU. Rubric: observed library behavior; measured norms and their dependence on data; derivative explanation; distinction between error correction and gradient learning.",
  "solution": "<p>Record the actual result of backward() on torch.heaviside in your installed PyTorch version, including any unsupported-derivative error. Mathematically the hard step is locally constant away from zero and has no ordinary derivative at zero, so it gives no useful ordinary gradient for classification error. With sigmoid(kz), the local derivative is kσ(kz)(1−σ(kz)); it concentrates near z=0 as k grows. Gradients can shrink for points far from zero and can increase near zero; report actual norms rather than requiring a universal monotonic trend. The perceptron update is supervised error correction, not gradient descent on 0/1 error. Hidden-layer training needs usable derivatives, including piecewise derivatives for ReLU. Rubric: observed library behavior; measured norms and their dependence on data; derivative explanation; distinction between error correction and gradient learning.</p>"
},
