M3: {
  "statement": "**M3 · Stretch · mixed — changing a learned task.** Start with w=(2,1), b=−3, which computes AND. Switch the targets to OR and use η=1, presenting (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,1) in order. Count the updates before the first clean epoch and give the final parameters. Compare changing real-valued weights with flipping B-type modifiers: does a small parameter change guarantee a small change in hard-threshold predictions?",
  "plain": "From the stated AND weights, (0,0) is correctly silent. At (0,1), the score is −2 but OR requires 1, so the update gives w=(2,2), b=−2. The remaining two inputs are now correct, and the next epoch is clean. Thus there is one mistaken-example update before the clean epoch. Real-valued parameters allow increments smaller than a whole switch flip, but hard-threshold predictions can still jump when a score crosses zero. Small parameter movement alone does not guarantee small changes in the output labels; examples near the boundary are sensitive. Rubric: score before the update; final weights and bias; one update and a clean-epoch check; distinction between graded parameters and discontinuous predictions.",
  "solution": "<p>From the stated AND weights, (0,0) is correctly silent. At (0,1), the score is −2 but OR requires 1, so the update gives w=(2,2), b=−2. The remaining two inputs are now correct, and the next epoch is clean. Thus there is one mistaken-example update before the clean epoch. Real-valued parameters allow increments smaller than a whole switch flip, but hard-threshold predictions can still jump when a score crosses zero. Small parameter movement alone does not guarantee small changes in the output labels; examples near the boundary are sensitive. Rubric: score before the update; final weights and bias; one update and a clean-epoch check; distinction between graded parameters and discontinuous predictions.</p>"
},
M4: {
  "statement": "**M4 · Stretch · mixed — is Hebb's rule a perceptron rule?** Write the perceptron update as a Hebbian product of an input and an error signal. Explain the role of the teacher signal d, and why plain Hebbian learning with y in place of d−y cannot learn to stop. Then distinguish Oja's response-dependent shrinkage from supervised error correction: which rule uses a teacher, and which can fluctuate with a fixed step size?",
  "plain": "The perceptron update η(d−y)x has the form input times an error signal; the teacher target d tells the rule whether the response was correct. On a correct example the update is zero. Plain Hebbian ηyx can keep reinforcing a nonzero linear response even when no task error is being corrected. Oja subtracts ηy²w, balancing reinforcement against response-dependent shrinkage without a teacher. This can stabilize the mean-field direction and norm under the stated assumptions; fixed-step stochastic weights can still fluctuate. Neither Oja nor plain Hebb is supervised error correction, and bounded weights alone do not establish classification accuracy. Rubric: product form; teacher versus no teacher; mechanism of competition; distinction between mean-field stabilization and discrete stochastic behavior.",
  "solution": "<p>The perceptron update η(d−y)x has the form input times an error signal; the teacher target d tells the rule whether the response was correct. On a correct example the update is zero. Plain Hebbian ηyx can keep reinforcing a nonzero linear response even when no task error is being corrected. Oja subtracts ηy²w, balancing reinforcement against response-dependent shrinkage without a teacher. This can stabilize the mean-field direction and norm under the stated assumptions; fixed-step stochastic weights can still fluctuate. Neither Oja nor plain Hebb is supervised error correction, and bounded weights alone do not establish classification accuracy. Rubric: product form; teacher versus no teacher; mechanism of competition; distinction between mean-field stabilization and discrete stochastic behavior.</p>"
},
K1: {
  "statement": "**K1 · Foundation · gate — name the parts.** Define a threshold unit, its pre-activation and its decision boundary. Say what makes a function linear rather than affine, and which of the two a perceptron applies its threshold to.",
  "plain": "A threshold unit computes z=w·x+b, the pre-activation, and outputs 1 when z≥0 and 0 otherwise. Its decision boundary is the set w·x+b=0, a hyperplane with normal w when w≠0; at w=0 the output is constant. A linear function sends 0 to 0 and preserves sums and scalar multiples, as x↦w·x does. An affine function is a linear function plus a constant, as x↦w·x+b is. A perceptron applies its threshold to an affine function. Rubric: correct definitions of pre-activation and boundary; the distinction between linear and affine; the statement that the threshold acts on the affine map.",
  "solution": "<p>A threshold unit computes z=w·x+b, the pre-activation, and outputs 1 when z≥0 and 0 otherwise. Its decision boundary is the set w·x+b=0, a hyperplane with normal w when w≠0; at w=0 the output is constant. A linear function sends 0 to 0 and preserves sums and scalar multiples, as x↦w·x does. An affine function is a linear function plus a constant, as x↦w·x+b is. A perceptron applies its threshold to an affine function. Rubric: correct definitions of pre-activation and boundary; the distinction between linear and affine; the statement that the threshold acts on the affine map.</p>"
},
K2: {
  "statement": "**K2 · Stretch · gate — prove a separation result.** Prove that no single threshold unit computes \"x₁ and x₂ are equal\" on two Boolean inputs, then give a network of three threshold units that does, with weights and thresholds.",
  "plain": "Suppose one unit with weights w₁, w₂ and threshold T computes equality. Then (0,0) fires, so 0≥T; (1,1) fires, so w₁+w₂≥T; (1,0) and (0,1) are silent, so w₁<T and w₂<T. Adding the last two gives w₁+w₂<2T. Since T≤0, 2T≤T, so w₁+w₂<T, contradicting the (1,1) row. A network: h₁=AND(x₁,x₂) with weights 1, 1 and threshold 2; h₂=NOR(x₁,x₂) with weights −1, −1 and threshold 0; output OR(h₁,h₂) with weights 1, 1 and threshold 1. Check: (0,0) fires h₂, (1,1) fires h₁, and the mixed inputs fire neither. Rubric: all four inequalities; the sign of T from (0,0); the contradiction; a three-unit network checked on all four inputs.",
  "solution": "<p>Suppose one unit with weights w₁, w₂ and threshold T computes equality. Then (0,0) fires, so 0≥T; (1,1) fires, so w₁+w₂≥T; (1,0) and (0,1) are silent, so w₁<T and w₂<T. Adding the last two gives w₁+w₂<2T. Since T≤0, 2T≤T, so w₁+w₂<T, contradicting the (1,1) row. A network: h₁=AND(x₁,x₂) with weights 1, 1 and threshold 2; h₂=NOR(x₁,x₂) with weights −1, −1 and threshold 0; output OR(h₁,h₂) with weights 1, 1 and threshold 1. Check: (0,0) fires h₂, (1,1) fires h₁, and the mixed inputs fire neither. Rubric: all four inequalities; the sign of T from (0,0); the contradiction; a three-unit network checked on all four inputs.</p>"
},
